<h2>Sand Point Way Can Be Better</h2>

<p>
  Sand Point Way NE runs through northeast Seattle, connecting the University
  District to Magnuson Park and beyond.  For decades it served as State Route
  513 &mdash; a designation that prioritised cars over everyone else.  The
  highway designation is gone, but the road culture has not caught up.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Beyond SR 513</strong> is a community advocacy effort pushing for
  safer crossings, protected bike lanes, and pedestrian-friendly streetscaping
  along Sand Point Way NE.  We believe a road that once moved state highway
  traffic can be redesigned to move people &mdash; on foot, on bikes, and in
  transit &mdash; while remaining a useful corridor for cars.
</p>

<h2>The Problem</h2>

<p>
  Sand Point Way NE has long stretches with no safe crossing, narrow or
  non-existent sidewalks, and fast-moving traffic that makes walking or
  cycling feel dangerous.  Nearby destinations &mdash; Magnuson Park, Children&rsquo;s
  Hospital, the Burke-Gilman Trail, and dozens of residential neighbourhoods
  &mdash; deserve better connections.
</p>

<h2>Our Vision</h2>

<ul style="margin: 0 0 1rem 1.5rem;">
  <li>Protected pedestrian crossings at every major intersection</li>
  <li>Continuous sidewalks on both sides of the road</li>
  <li>A protected bike lane connecting the Burke-Gilman Trail to Magnuson Park</li>
  <li>Street trees and traffic calming to reduce vehicle speeds</li>
  <li>Better bus stops and shelters for Metro Route 75</li>
</ul>

